  "notes": "⚠ **This is a top-line model, not a beat model** — the research says outright that he is not the rhythm source — so the drums stay close to the parent and everything authored sits in `melody`, `countermelody` and `bassline`. What is his: 2- or 4-bar melodic cells (`phraseBars` weighted evenly across both, which also keeps trap 6 satisfied), small intervals with one leap per phrase (`step` 0.6 / `third` 0.32 / `leap` 0.08), long held notes across 2 and 4 so `densityPerBar` runs 2–5, and self-harmonising in stacked 3rds and octaves over 2–4 tracks — which is `octave_echo` at weight 4 with `densityRatio` 0.6, the densest counter on this axis. Gospel-choir background drives `blues` and `minor_pentatonic` to the top of the scale weights with the blue-3 and flat-7 the research names. ⚠ **`melody.register` 50–74 is inferred**: the research gives a tenor-to-baritone voice and no numbers, so this is the standard tenor span rather than a measurement, and it is the parameter to correct first. ⚠ `timbreHint` is `vocal_chant`, the nearest token in this dataset's existing vocabulary for a sung hook line — none of the available hints describe a lead vocal. ⚠ The bass matters here for an unusual reason: \"hooks are written against the bassline rather than the chords\", contour mirroring or answering it in contrary motion, so the bass is authored melodic (`independent_riff`, `followRootsProb` 0.42) rather than as an accompaniment. ⚠ `confidence` is `medium`, matching the research's high-medium rating. The arrangement opens on the hook because the research says he frequently opens the record before any rap enters, and the 4-bar pre-hook is modelled as a bridge because this engine's section vocabulary has no pre-hook at this length.",
